      'title' => 'Company Formation in KSA: Which Structure Fits Your Business?',
      'path_alias' => '/blog/company-formation-in-ksa-which-structure-fits-your-business',
      'body' => '<h1>Company Formation in KSA: Choosing the Right Business Structure</h1>
<p>Selecting the wrong business structure during <strong>company formation in KSA</strong> costs investors months of delays, regulatory headaches, and unnecessary capital requirements. The Kingdom\'s Ministry of Commerce offers multiple entity types, each with distinct ownership rules, liability frameworks, and compliance obligations. Your structure determines everything from minimum capital to foreign ownership percentages and taxation.</p>
<p>Understanding these differences before registration prevents costly restructuring later. This guide compares every available structure for commercial activities in Saudi Arabia, helping you select the entity that aligns with your operational needs, investor profile, and growth strategy.</p>
<h2>Why Your Business Structure Choice Matters for Company Formation in KSA</h2>
<p>Most foreign investors default to the <strong>Limited Liability Company (LLC)</strong> without evaluating alternatives. That decision impacts your daily operations for years.</p>
<p>Your chosen structure dictates who can own shares, how profits are distributed, and whether you need Saudi partners. It determines your <strong>Commercial Registration (CR)</strong> category, <strong>ZATCA</strong> tax filing requirements, and <strong>Nitaqat Saudization</strong> quotas.</p>
<p>The wrong structure limits access to government contracts, restricts expansion into certain sectors, and complicates exit strategies. For example, branch offices cannot sell products directly in Saudi markets—they\'re restricted to representing their foreign parent company.</p>
<h3>Regulatory Authority and Approval Process</h3>
<p>The <strong>Ministry of Commerce</strong> oversees all commercial entity registrations through digital platforms. <strong>MISA (Ministry of Investment of Saudi Arabia)</strong> handles foreign investment licenses for most sectors, though some activities require additional ministry approvals.</p>

<p>Each structure follows different approval pathways. Single-person companies receive instant digital approval. Joint stock companies require ministerial review. Understanding your structure\'s regulatory route prevents application rejections.</p>
<h2>The Four Primary Structures for Commercial Company Registration KSA</h2>
<p>Saudi commercial law recognizes multiple entity types, but four dominate foreign and domestic investment. Each serves different investor profiles and business models.</p>
<p>Choosing between them requires understanding capital requirements, liability exposure, and operational flexibility. The table below compares core characteristics across all four structures.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th><strong>Structure</strong></th>
<th><strong>Minimum Capital</strong></th>
<th><strong>Foreign Ownership</strong></th>
<th><strong>Shareholders</strong></th>
<th><strong>Liability</strong></th>
<th><strong>Best For</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>LLC</strong></td>
<td>SAR 1 (no minimum)</td>
<td>Up to 100%</td>
<td>2–50 partners</td>
<td>Limited</td>
<td>SMEs, service firms, consulting</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Single-Person Company</strong></td>
<td>SAR 1 (no minimum)</td>
<td>100% allowed</td>
<td>1 owner only</td>
<td>Limited</td>
<td>Freelancers, sole proprietors</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>JSC (Closed)</strong></td>
<td>SAR 500,000</td>
<td>Up to 100%</td>
<td>2–200 shareholders</td>
<td>Limited</td>
<td>Large ventures, family businesses</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Branch Office</strong></td>
<td>No capital deposit</td>
<td>100% foreign</td>
<td>Parent company</td>
<td>Parent liable</td>
<td>Market research, liaison activities</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Capital Requirements Have Changed</h3>
<p>Recent regulatory reforms eliminated minimum capital requirements for LLCs and single-person companies. You can now establish either entity with symbolic SAR 1 capital.</p>
<p>However, banks still require practical operating capital for account opening. <strong>Commercial Registration</strong> authorities may question unrealistically low capitalization for capital-intensive activities like construction or manufacturing.</p>

<p>Joint stock companies maintain the SAR 500,000 minimum. This threshold signals regulatory intent—JSCs are designed for substantial commercial operations, not small-scale ventures.</p>
<h2>LLC Formation Saudi Arabia: The Default Choice Explained</h2>
<p><strong>Limited Liability Companies</strong> dominate foreign investment structures. They balance operational flexibility with straightforward compliance, making them suitable for most commercial activities.</p>
<p>An LLC requires at least two partners—individuals or corporate entities. Maximum partner count is fifty. Partners draft <strong>articles of association</strong> defining ownership percentages, profit distribution, and management authority.</p>
<h3>Ownership and Management Flexibility</h3>
<p>LLCs permit 100% foreign ownership in most sectors under MISA\'s negative list framework. Restricted sectors—like recruitment, commercial agencies, and certain services—require Saudi partners holding minimum ownership percentages.</p>
<p>Management can be partner-led or delegated to appointed managers. This flexibility accommodates both hands-on investors and passive capital contributors seeking operational partners.</p>
<p>For example, a foreign investor setting up an LLC in Riyadh for management consulting would typically establish 100% ownership, appoint themselves as managing partner, and hire Saudi staff to meet Nitaqat requirements. Motaded structures these arrangements to satisfy both ownership regulations and Saudization quotas—motaded.com.sa.</p>
<h3>Liability Protection and Legal Status</h3>
<p>Partners face limited liability—they\'re only responsible for company debts up to their capital contribution. Personal assets remain protected unless partners provide personal guarantees for loans or contracts.</p>
<p>The LLC itself holds independent legal personality. It can own property, sign contracts, sue and be sued under its registered commercial name. This separation protects individual partners from business liabilities.</p>
<p>ZATCA treats LLCs as resident corporate entities, subject to 20% corporate income tax on adjusted net profits. VAT registration becomes mandatory once taxable supplies exceed SAR 375,000 annually.</p>
<h2>Single-Person Company: When Solo Ownership Makes Sense</h2>
<p>The <strong>Single-Person Company</strong> structure serves individual entrepreneurs wanting full ownership without partnership obligations. It functions identically to an LLC but with one shareholder.</p>
<p>This structure suits freelancers, consultants, and small business owners transitioning from informal arrangements to formal corporate status. It provides liability protection and corporate credibility impossible with sole proprietorships.</p>
<h3>Registration Requirements and Limitations</h3>
<p>Single-person companies follow streamlined registration through the Ministry of Commerce portal. Approval is typically instant if the business name passes uniqueness checks and sector restrictions.</p>
<p>The sole owner cannot establish multiple single-person companies. Saudi regulations limit each individual to one such entity—preventing proliferation of undercapitalized shells. However, the same individual can own shares in LLCs or JSCs simultaneously.</p>

<p>Foreign nationals can establish single-person companies with 100% ownership in open sectors. The same MISA foreign investment license requirements apply as for LLCs.</p>
<h3>When to Choose Single-Person Over LLC</h3>
<p>If you\'re a solo entrepreneur without partners, this structure eliminates partnership governance complexity. You control all decisions without shareholder meetings, voting requirements, or profit-sharing negotiations.</p>
<p>However, single-person companies face perception challenges when bidding on large contracts. Government tenders and corporate procurement often favor multi-partner entities, viewing them as more stable and resourced.</p>
<p>The structure also limits capital-raising options. You cannot sell shares to raise funds—equity expansion requires converting to an LLC or JSC. Growth-focused ventures should consider partnership structures from inception.</p>
<h2>Joint Stock Companies: For Large-Scale Operations</h2>
<p><strong>Closed Joint Stock Companies (JSC)</strong> serve substantial commercial ventures requiring significant capitalization or accommodating numerous shareholders. The SAR 500,000 minimum capital requirement reflects their intended scale.</p>
<p>JSCs can have two to two hundred shareholders. Shares are freely transferable among existing shareholders unless articles of association impose restrictions. This transferability facilitates family business succession and investor entry or exit.</p>
<h3>Governance Structure and Compliance Burden</h3>
<p>JSCs require formal governance: a board of directors, regular shareholder assemblies, and audited financial statements. Board meetings must follow documented procedures with recorded minutes.</p>
<p>The Ministry of Commerce mandates annual general assemblies where shareholders approve financial statements, elect directors, and authorize major transactions. These compliance requirements generate ongoing administrative costs.</p>
<p>Working with Motaded at incorporation means establishing governance frameworks that satisfy regulatory requirements while remaining practical for your operational reality—motaded.com.sa.</p>
<p>Companies planning public listing or seeking institutional investment should establish as JSCs. The structure accommodates future capital increases and complex ownership arrangements impossible in LLCs.</p>
<h3>When JSC Structure Justifies the Complexity</h3>
<p>Family businesses transferring across generations benefit from JSC share structures. Parents can distribute ownership among children while maintaining centralized board management. Share transfers avoid gift tax complications.</p>
<p>Ventures requiring capital well beyond SAR 500,000 gain credibility from JSC status. Major construction projects, manufacturing facilities, and infrastructure ventures use JSC structures to signal financial substance to partners and customers.</p>
<p>Technology startups seeking venture capital often establish as JSCs. The structure accommodates multiple funding rounds with new shareholders while protecting existing ownership through documented share classes and transfer restrictions.</p>
<h2>Branch Office Registration: Limited But Strategic Uses</h2>
<p><strong>Branch offices</strong> represent foreign companies in Saudi Arabia without creating separate legal entities. The foreign parent company remains liable for all branch activities and obligations.</p>
<p>Branches cannot engage in commercial trading—they\'re restricted to liaison, market research, representing the parent in negotiations, and contract support. This limitation makes them unsuitable for most business models but valuable for specific strategic purposes.</p>
<h3>Operational Restrictions and Compliance</h3>
<p>Branches require MISA foreign investment licenses specifying permitted activities. These licenses typically authorize support functions like marketing, customer service, and parent company representation, but prohibit direct sales or service delivery to Saudi customers.</p>
<p>The branch operates under the parent company\'s name with "Branch" designation. It cannot enter contracts independently—all agreements are technically between Saudi parties and the foreign parent company.</p>

<p>All branch revenues flow to the foreign parent. Saudi tax authorities assess withholding obligations based on permanent establishment rules under relevant tax treaties. Most branches pay withholding tax on Saudi-sourced income rather than corporate income tax.</p>
<h3>Strategic Use Cases for Branch Structures</h3>
<p>Multinational corporations bidding on government contracts often establish branches to satisfy local presence requirements without incorporating separate subsidiaries. The branch can sign contracts on behalf of the foreign parent while maintaining unified global operations.</p>
<p>Service companies supporting existing customers in Saudi Arabia use branches for after-sales service, training, and technical support. These activities qualify as permissible branch functions without triggering commercial trading restrictions.</p>
This staged approach minimizes upfront capital requirements and regulatory complexity during market testing phases.</p>
<h2>Step-by-Step: Choosing Your Optimal Structure</h2>
<p>Selecting the right entity requires matching structure characteristics to your specific business model, investor profile, and growth trajectory. Follow this decision framework to narrow options systematically.</p>
<h3>Step 1: Assess Ownership Requirements</h3>
<p>Count your partners. Solo operators choose single-person companies or LLCs with a trusted partner. Multiple investors need LLCs or JSCs depending on shareholder count and governance preferences.</p>
<p>Determine foreign ownership percentages. Check MISA\'s foreign investment negative list for your sector. Some activities remain closed or restricted to Saudi nationals or GCC citizens.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Evaluate Capital and Credibility Needs</h3>
<p>Calculate realistic operating capital. While minimum requirements are nominal, your bank, landlords, and customers expect capital appropriate to your business scale. Undercapitalization damages credibility.</p>
<p>Consider market perception. Large-scale ventures gain legitimacy from JSC structures and substantial stated capital. Small service businesses operate effectively as LLCs without perception penalties.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Analyze Activity Types and Restrictions</h3>
<p>Review your planned activities against each structure\'s operational permissions. Trading requires commercial entities—LLCs, single-person companies, or JSCs. Branch offices cannot conduct direct commerce.</p>
<p>Identify sector-specific requirements. Some activities mandate particular structures. For example, recruitment agencies often face ownership restrictions requiring Saudi partners, making wholly foreign-owned single-person companies impossible.</p>
<h3>Step 4: Project Growth and Exit Scenarios</h3>
<p>Anticipate future capital raises. Ventures planning multiple funding rounds need JSC flexibility for new shareholders and complex share classes. LLCs accommodate limited expansion but become cumbersome beyond ten partners.</p>
<p>Consider exit strategies. Share sales are simpler in JSCs than LLC partner exits, which require articles of association amendments and partner approvals. Buyers often prefer JSC acquisitions for cleaner ownership transfers.</p>
<h3>Step 5: Calculate Compliance Costs and Administrative Capacity</h3>
<p>Budget for ongoing requirements. JSCs incur auditor fees, board meeting costs, and assembly administration. LLCs have lighter compliance burdens, reducing operational overhead for smaller ventures.</p>
<p>Motaded provides compliance management for all structures, handling annual filings, assembly documentation, and regulatory submissions regardless of entity type—motaded.com.sa.</p>
<h2>Common Structure Selection Mistakes That Cost Time and Money</h2>
<p>Many investors choose structures based on incomplete information or generic advice. These errors create operational friction and expensive restructuring needs.</p>
<h3>Mistake 1: Defaulting to LLC Without Evaluating Alternatives</h3>
<p>The LLC\'s popularity doesn\'t make it universally optimal. Solo entrepreneurs waste time and complexity maintaining two-partner LLCs when single-person companies better fit their needs.</p>
<p>Conversely, ventures planning substantial capitalization or seeking investment underestimate JSC benefits. They establish LLCs, then restructure to JSCs when raising capital, duplicating registration costs and creating contractual complications with existing agreements under the old entity.</p>
<h3>Mistake 2: Ignoring Sector-Specific Ownership Rules</h3>
<p>Investors assume 100% foreign ownership applies universally. They design business models around wholly-owned entities, only discovering during application that their sector requires Saudi partners.</p>

<h3>Mistake 3: Underestimating Branch Office Limitations</h3>
<p>Foreign companies establish branches expecting full commercial operations. They discover too late that branches cannot invoice Saudi customers directly or conduct trading activities.</p>
<p>The resulting workaround—channeling sales through the parent company—creates pricing complications, tax inefficiencies, and customer confusion. Proper structure selection means choosing commercial entities when trading is central to your business model.</p>
<h3>Mistake 4: Overlooking Future Operational Needs</h3>
<p>Businesses choose structures based solely on launch requirements, ignoring growth trajectories. They optimize for speed and cost at incorporation, creating constraints that limit expansion within years.</p>
<p>For example, technology ventures establishing as LLCs discover that investor due diligence for Series A funding requires governance formality impossible in partnership structures. They convert to JSCs mid-fundraise, delaying closes and introducing execution risk.</p>
<h2>How MISA Investment Licenses Interact With Company Structures</h2>
<p><strong>MISA (Ministry of Investment of Saudi Arabia)</strong> issues foreign investment licenses authorizing non-Saudi ownership in commercial entities. Your structure selection determines license requirements and approval timelines.</p>
<p>All foreign-owned LLCs, single-person companies, and JSCs require MISA licenses before commercial registration. Branch offices also need MISA approval despite being extensions of foreign entities rather than separate companies.</p>
<h3>The Negative List and Sector Restrictions</h3>
<p>MISA maintains a negative list specifying closed and restricted sectors. Closed sectors prohibit foreign investment entirely—examples include certain religious tourism services and specific natural resource activities.</p>
<p>Restricted sectors permit foreign investment with conditions: minimum Saudi ownership percentages, partnership with Saudi entities, or special licensing requirements beyond standard MISA approval.</p>
<p>Your structure choice must accommodate these restrictions. A sector requiring 25% Saudi ownership eliminates single-person company options and mandates LLC or JSC structures with Saudi partners at the required percentage.</p>
<h3>Licensing Process and Timeline</h3>
<p>MISA licenses for standard commercial activities in open sectors typically process within days through the online portal. Restricted sectors require additional documentation and ministry coordination, extending timelines to weeks.</p>
<p>Motaded submits MISA applications simultaneously with structure selection, ensuring license approval aligns with commercial registration readiness—motaded.com.sa.</p>
<p>License conditions specify permitted activities using ISIC classification codes. These activity codes must match your planned operations exactly—mismatches trigger registration delays or compliance violations post-launch.</p>
<h2>Capital Requirements and Banking Considerations Across Structures</h2>
<p>While regulatory minimum capital is nominal for most structures, practical banking requirements demand realistic capitalization. Banks assess capital adequacy during account opening, often requiring deposits far exceeding statutory minimums.</p>
<h3>Bank Account Opening Standards</h3>
<p>Saudi banks evaluate your business model, anticipated transaction volumes, and operational needs when opening accounts. They expect capital sufficient to sustain operations during early months before revenue generation.</p>
<p>Service businesses typically need SAR 50,000–100,000 in initial deposits. Trading businesses require higher amounts reflecting inventory financing needs. Manufacturing ventures may need SAR 500,000 or more regardless of legal structure.</p>
<p>Banks also consider your structure\'s credibility. JSCs with substantial stated capital receive preferential treatment and better banking terms than minimally capitalized LLCs, even when actual deposits are comparable.</p>
<h3>Capital Contribution Documentation</h3>
<p>Your articles of association state registered capital and partner contributions. These amounts become public record in your commercial registration documents.</p>
<p>Partners must document capital transfers into company accounts. Banks require proof that stated capital actually exists—wire transfer confirmations from partners into the company account bearing formation costs and initial operations.</p>

<p>Undocumented capital—where articles state SAR 500,000 capital but bank accounts show SAR 50,000—creates audit vulnerabilities and undermines credibility with suppliers and customers reviewing your commercial registration.</p>
<h2>Articles of Association: Your Structure\'s Operating Manual</h2>
<p><strong>Articles of association</strong> define your entity\'s governance, management authority, profit distribution, and dispute resolution mechanisms. They function as your company\'s constitution, governing partner relationships and operational procedures.</p>
<p>Every structure except branch offices requires articles of association filed with commercial registration. These documents must comply with Saudi commercial law while addressing your specific operational needs.</p>
<h3>Essential Provisions Across All Structures</h3>
<p>Articles must specify company name, headquarters location, business activities, capital amount, and partner contributions. They define management structure—who has signing authority, spending limits, and contract approval rights.</p>
<p>Profit and loss distribution formulas appear in articles. While ownership percentages typically determine distributions, partners can negotiate performance-based allocations, salary arrangements, or reinvestment requirements different from ownership ratios.</p>
<p>Admission of new partners or shareholders requires procedures detailed in articles. These provisions determine whether existing partners have preemptive rights to purchase new capital increases and what approval percentages govern ownership changes.</p>
<h3>Dispute Resolution and Exit Mechanisms</h3>
<p>Well-drafted articles include partner dispute resolution procedures: mediation requirements, arbitration clauses, and deadlock-breaking mechanisms. These provisions prevent operational paralysis when partners disagree on major decisions.</p>
<p>Exit procedures define partner withdrawal or forced exit conditions. They establish company or partner purchase rights, valuation methodologies for departing partners\' shares, and payment terms for buyouts.</p>
<p>For example, articles might grant companies first right to purchase a departing partner\'s shares at book value, with remaining partners having secondary purchase rights if the company declines.
<h3>Structure-Specific Provisions</h3>
<p>LLC articles address management authority distribution among partners. They can designate managing partners with operational control or require unanimous consent for major transactions.</p>
<p>JSC articles define board composition, director election procedures, and shareholder assembly rules. They specify quorum requirements, voting thresholds for different decision types, and director liability limitations.</p>
<p>Single-person company articles are simpler, focusing on activity scope, management procedures, and succession planning if the sole owner becomes incapacitated or dies.</p>
<h2>Compliance Timeline: From Structure Selection to Operations</h2>
<p>Understanding post-incorporation compliance prevents regulatory penalties and operational disruptions. Your structure determines filing requirements, audit obligations, and reporting deadlines.</p>
<h3>Immediate Post-Registration Requirements</h3>
<p>After commercial registration issuance, companies must complete <strong>ZATCA</strong> tax registration for VAT and income tax purposes. This registration occurs through ZATCA\'s online portal, linking your CR number to tax obligations.</p>
<p>Social insurance registration with <strong>GOSI</strong> becomes mandatory before hiring employees. Even single-person companies hiring their first employee must register and begin monthly contribution payments.</p>
<p><strong>Nitaqat</strong> registration with the Ministry of Human Resources occurs when your Saudi employee count requires Saudization compliance. Small entities enjoy grace periods, but registration requirements trigger at specific headcount thresholds.</p>
<h3>Ongoing Annual Compliance</h3>
<p>All structures except branch offices file annual tax returns with ZATCA. LLCs, single-person companies, and JSCs pay 20% corporate income tax on adjusted profits after allowable deductions.</p>
<p>JSCs must hold annual general assemblies within six months of fiscal year-end. Audited financial statements require shareholder approval at these assemblies. Minutes must be filed with the Ministry of Commerce.</p>
<p>Commercial registration renewal occurs annually. Companies pay renewal fees and confirm business activity, management, and ownership information remains current. Failure to renew results in CR suspension and operational prohibition.</p>
<h2>Sector-Specific Structure Considerations</h2>
<p>Certain industries face unique structure requirements or restrictions. Understanding these sector rules prevents application rejections and ensures long-term operational compliance.</p>
<h3>Professional Services and Licensed Activities</h3>
<p>Consulting, legal services, accounting, and engineering require professional licensing beyond commercial registration. License-issuing authorities often mandate specific ownership structures or Saudi partner participation.</p>
<p>For instance, legal consultancies typically require Saudi attorney ownership percentages regardless of broader foreign investment liberalization. Motaded navigates these professional licensing requirements alongside standard company formation—motaded.com.sa.</p>
<h3>Trading and Distribution Activities</h3>
<p>Import-export businesses and commercial agencies face ownership restrictions in certain product categories. While general trading is open to 100% foreign ownership, representing specific international brands as commercial agents may require Saudi participation.</p>
<p>These restrictions reflect commercial agency law protecting Saudi distributors.
<h3>Manufacturing and Industrial Projects</h3>
<p>Manufacturing ventures often establish as JSCs to accommodate substantial capital requirements and facilitate future expansion. Industrial projects benefit from JSC credibility when negotiating utility connections, industrial land allocation, and bank financing.</p>
JSC structures signal commitment and scale that improves access to incentives and industrial infrastructure.</p>
<h2>Regional Considerations: How Location Affects Structure Choice</h2>
<p>Saudi Arabia\'s different regions offer varying business environments, free zone benefits, and regulatory approaches. Your structure selection should account for geographic operational factors.</p>
<h3>Riyadh: Administrative and Government Contract Focus</h3>
<p>Riyadh-based businesses frequently target government contracts and public sector clients. These opportunities often favor established JSC structures with substantial stated capital over smaller LLCs.</p>
<p>The capital\'s concentration of ministry headquarters and regulatory authorities means Riyadh entities face more stringent scrutiny during inspections and audits. Comprehensive compliance becomes critical regardless of structure.</p>
<h3>Eastern Province: Industrial and Energy Sectors</h3>
<p>Eastern Province\'s petrochemical and industrial clusters attract manufacturing ventures and energy services. These capital-intensive businesses typically establish as JSCs to match sector credibility expectations.</p>

<h3>Special Economic Zones and Free Zones</h3>
<p>Free zones offer modified regulatory frameworks including expedited licensing, customs benefits, and operational flexibility. Some zones permit structures unavailable in mainland Saudi Arabia, though recent reforms have narrowed these differences.</p>
<p>Zone-specific regulations may affect minimum capital requirements, ownership restrictions, or compliance obligations.
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3>Q: Can I change my business structure after registration in Saudi Arabia?</h3>
<p><strong>A:</strong> Yes, structure conversion is possible but requires Ministry of Commerce approval, articles of association amendments, and updated commercial registration. The process takes several weeks and incurs legal, registration, and potential tax costs. Choosing correctly at formation avoids these complications and expenses.</p>
<h3>Q: Do all foreign investors need Saudi partners for company formation in KSA?</h3>
<p><strong>A:</strong> No, most sectors permit 100% foreign ownership under MISA\'s negative list framework. Restricted sectors require Saudi partners at specified percentages. Your specific business activity determines ownership rules. MISA\'s online portal provides sector-specific guidance, though professional verification prevents misinterpretation and application errors.</p>
<h3>Q: What is the fastest business structure to register in Saudi Arabia?</h3>
<p><strong>A:</strong> Single-person companies receive the fastest approval—often instant through digital channels if business names pass uniqueness checks. LLCs typically approve within days. JSCs require longer ministry review due to governance complexity and higher capital requirements. Branch offices need MISA approval coordinating with parent company documentation.</p>
