<h2>MISA Investment License: The Foundation Step</h2>
<p>The <strong>MISA investment license</strong> represents your legal authorization to establish a foreign-invested entity in Saudi Arabia. No subsequent registration steps can proceed without MISA approval.</p>
<p>MISA evaluates your proposed business activities against the <strong>Negative List</strong>—sectors restricted or prohibited for foreign investment. Most sectors now permit 100% foreign ownership, but certain activities require Saudi partnership or remain fully closed to foreign capital.</p>
<h3>Step-by-Step MISA Application Process</h3>
<p><strong>Step 1: Create MISA Portal Account</strong>
Register at the MISA investor portal using your corporate email. Verify your account through the two-factor authentication system. Designate an authorized representative with power of attorney if applying on behalf of a corporate investor.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Complete Investment Proposal</strong>
Detail your proposed business activities using the official ISIC classification codes. Specify your legal structure, intended capital investment, projected employment numbers, and planned business locations. MISA requires explicit description of how your investment aligns with Vision 2030 priorities.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Upload Required Documentation</strong>
Submit authenticated corporate documents, investor identification, financial statements, and business plan materials. Each document must meet specific file format and size requirements. The portal validates documents automatically and flags any missing items.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Pay Application Fees</strong>
MISA charges fees based on your requested capital level and business activities. Payment processes through SADAD or direct bank transfer. Retain payment confirmation for tracking purposes.</p>
<p><strong>Step 5: Await Review and Approval</strong>
MISA typically reviews applications within 2-4 weeks. Complex cases or restricted sectors may require additional scrutiny. The portal notifies you of approval, requests for additional information, or rejection with specific reasons.</p>


<h2>Commercial Registration: Establishing Your Legal Entity</h2>
<p>With MISA approval secured, you proceed to register your commercial entity with the <strong>Ministry of Commerce</strong>. The <strong>Commercial Registration (CR)</strong> serves as your company\'s legal identity and business authorization document.</p>
<p>The CR process involves multiple sequential steps within the Ministry of Commerce portal system. Each step must be completed precisely in order, as the system locks subsequent stages until prior requirements are fulfilled.</p>
<h3>Name Reservation and Approval</h3>
<p>Begin by reserving your company name through the Ministry of Commerce portal. Saudi Arabia requires commercial names in Arabic, though you may register an English trade name separately.</p>
<p>Your proposed name must comply with naming conventions: it cannot resemble existing registered entities, include prohibited terms, or suggest activities outside your licensed scope. The system performs automated similarity checks against the commercial registry database.</p>
<p>Submit three name preferences ranked by priority. The Ministry typically approves name reservations within 24-48 hours. Approved names remain reserved for 60 days, during which you must complete subsequent CR steps.</p>
<h3>Articles of Association Drafting and Notarization</h3>
<p>Your <strong>Articles of Association (AoA)</strong> constitute your company\'s foundational legal document. For LLCs, the AoA must specify shareholders, capital contributions, management structure, profit distribution, and operational rules.</p>
<p>Saudi law mandates specific AoA clauses regarding capital, management authority, shareholder rights, and company duration. Your AoA must align precisely with your MISA-approved activities and stated capital structure.</p>
<p>Once drafted, your AoA requires notarization by an authorized Saudi notary public. Digital notarization is available through the Ministry of Justice portal for straightforward LLC structures. Complex arrangements or joint ventures may require in-person notarization.</p>
<h3>Ministry of Commerce CR Issuance</h3>
<p>Submit your notarized AoA, MISA approval, name reservation confirmation, and lease agreement for your registered address through the Ministry of Commerce portal. The system generates your CR application automatically.</p>
<p>The Ministry reviews your submission for consistency between MISA approval, AoA provisions, and CR application details. Upon approval, it issues your <strong>Commercial Registration Certificate</strong> with your unique CR number.</p>
<p>Your CR specifies your legal entity type, registered capital, authorized business activities (as ISIC codes), company address, and management details. This document is required for all subsequent licensing, banking, and regulatory interactions.</p>

