<h2>Labour and Employment Platform Registration</h2>
<p>Employment of staff requires registration on Saudi Arabia\'s unified labour platform <strong>Qiwa</strong> and compliance with <strong>Nitaqat Saudization</strong> requirements. These systems govern work visa issuance, employment contracts, and Saudi national employment quotas.</p>
<h3>Qiwa Platform Setup</h3>
<p>Register your company on Qiwa using your CR number and ZATCA registration details. The platform creates your employer profile automatically, linking to your commercial registration data.</p>
<p>Designate authorized users within your organization for recruitment, visa processing, and contract management. Qiwa operates on a role-based access system, allowing you to control which users perform specific functions.</p>
<p>Complete your company profile with details about your business activities, planned employment levels, and workplace characteristics. This information determines your Nitaqat classification and Saudi employment obligations.</p>
<h3>Nitaqat Saudization Compliance</h3>
<p>The <strong>Nitaqat program</strong> establishes mandatory Saudi national employment percentages based on company size and sector. New companies receive a grace period before Nitaqat obligations fully activate, typically ranging from one to two years.</p>
<p>Your Nitaqat band determines eligibility for government services, visa quota, and access to incentive programs. Companies failing to meet required percentages face sanctions including visa quota freezes and restrictions on commercial activities.</p>

<p>Plan Saudi recruitment strategies from company inception. Partner with HRDF (Human Resources Development Fund) programs offering wage subsidies for Saudi national employment in certain sectors.</p>
<h3>Work Visa Processing</h3>
<p>With Qiwa registration complete, you can initiate work visa applications for foreign employees. The system requires employment contracts, educational qualifications, professional experience verification, and health insurance coverage proof.</p>
<p>Saudi Arabia categorizes work visas by profession and nationality. Certain professions restrict foreign employment, reserving positions for Saudi nationals. Verify that your planned positions accept foreign workers before recruiting.</p>
<p>Medical examinations, biometric data collection, and background clearances occur after visa approval but before residence permit issuance. Factor these requirements into employee onboarding timelines.</p>

<h2>Sector-Specific Licenses and Regulatory Approvals</h2>
<p>Beyond core registration requirements, most foreign investors need sector-specific licenses from relevant regulatory authorities. These licenses authorize particular business activities and ensure compliance with industry regulations.</p>
<h3>Common Sector Regulators</h3>
<p><strong>Healthcare</strong>: Ministry of Health licenses medical facilities, pharmaceutical operations, and healthcare providers. Requirements include facility inspections, practitioner licensing, and quality management system certification.</p>
<p><strong>Financial Services</strong>: Saudi Central Bank (SAMA) regulates banking, insurance, and financial services. Authorization requires substantial capital, governance frameworks, and ongoing compliance reporting.</p>
<p><strong>Telecommunications</strong>: Communications, Space &amp; Technology Commission licenses telecom services, broadcasting, and digital platforms. Spectrum allocation and technical standards compliance are mandatory.</p>
<p><strong>Education</strong>: Ministry of Education authorizes educational institutions, training centers, and skill development operations. Curriculum approval and facility standards apply.</p>
<p><strong>Food and Beverage</strong>: Ministry of Environment, Water and Agriculture combined with Ministry of Health oversee food production, import, and service operations. Food safety certifications and regular inspections apply.</p>
<p><strong>Construction</strong>: Ministry of Municipal and Rural Affairs and Housing issues contractor classifications based on financial capacity and technical capabilities. Project value limits correspond to classification levels.</p>

<h3>Professional Service Licensing</h3>
<p>Professional services such as legal, accounting, engineering, and consulting require practitioners to hold valid Saudi licenses. Foreign professionals must obtain equivalency recognition for their qualifications through relevant Saudi professional bodies.</p>
<p>The Saudi Council of Engineers regulates engineering consultancies, requiring firm classification and individual engineer licensing. Similar requirements apply to legal consultancies through the Ministry of Justice and accounting firms through SOCPA (Saudi Organization for Chartered and Professional Accountants).</p>
<p>Obtain professional licenses before applying for your CR to ensure your commercial registration accurately reflects your authorized activities. Discrepancies between CR activities and professional licenses create compliance issues.</p>
<h2>Insurance and Risk Management Requirements</h2>
<p>Saudi regulations mandate specific insurance coverage for commercial operations. These requirements protect employees, customers, and third parties from various business risks.</p>
<h3>Mandatory Insurance Types</h3>
<p><strong>Medical Insurance</strong>: All employees must have health insurance meeting minimum coverage standards defined by the Council of Cooperative Health Insurance. Employers bear responsibility for providing and maintaining coverage.</p>
<p><strong>Worksite Insurance</strong>: Companies employing workers in industrial, construction, or high-risk environments must maintain occupational accident insurance covering workplace injuries and occupational diseases.</p>
<p><strong>Professional Indemnity</strong>: Regulated professions including healthcare, engineering, and legal services require professional indemnity insurance protecting clients against negligence or errors.</p>
<p><strong>Motor Vehicle Insurance</strong>: All company-owned vehicles require at least third-party liability coverage. Comprehensive insurance is advisable for valuable vehicle fleets.</p>
<h3>Obtaining Insurance Coverage</h3>
<p>Approach licensed Saudi insurance companies or cooperative insurance societies after CR issuance. Insurers require your CR certificate, business activity details, employee information, and risk assessment data.</p>
<p>Premium calculations consider your industry sector, number of employees, business activities, claim history, and risk management practices. Shopping multiple insurers ensures competitive pricing.</p>
<p>Policy issuance typically occurs within one week of application for standard coverage types. Specialized or high-risk operations may require longer underwriting processes.</p>
<p>Maintain continuous insurance coverage without lapses. Government platforms including Qiwa verify insurance status electronically, and coverage gaps trigger compliance violations.</p>

