      'body' => '<h1>Starting a Business in Saudi Arabia: What Every Founder Must Know</h1>
<p>Launching a business in Saudi Arabia requires more than capital and ambition. Foreign founders and local entrepreneurs must navigate legal structures, regulatory approvals, and strategic choices that shape their entire operation. Understanding <strong>what is branch office in saudi arabia</strong> alongside other entity types determines everything from ownership limits to tax obligations and expansion potential under Vision 2030.</p>
<p>The Kingdom\'s business ecosystem has transformed dramatically. New digital portals streamline company registration, foreign ownership rules have liberalized across sectors, and special economic zones offer unprecedented incentives. Yet the complexity remains substantial. Choosing between an <strong>LLC</strong>, representative office, or branch impacts your liability exposure, required capital, and operational freedom for years ahead.</p>
<h2>Understanding Saudi Arabia\'s Business Entity Landscape</h2>
<p>Foreign investors face five primary legal structures when starting operations. Each serves distinct strategic purposes and carries specific regulatory requirements.</p>
<p>The <strong>Limited Liability Company (LLC)</strong> dominates new registrations. It requires minimum capital of 1 SAR for Saudi-owned entities and allows 100% foreign ownership in most sectors since 2020. Shareholders enjoy liability protection limited to their capital contributions.</p>
<p><strong>Branch offices</strong> extend foreign parent companies into Saudi Arabia without creating separate legal entities. The parent company assumes full liability for branch operations. Branches suit established multinationals executing specific contracts or projects.</p>
