<h3>ZATCA Registration and Tax Compliance</h3>
<p><strong>ZATCA (Zakat, Tax and Customs Authority)</strong> registration occurs immediately after commercial registration. Your tax registration covers VAT obligations, corporate income tax for foreign-invested companies, and zakat for Gulf Cooperation Council national ownership.</p>
<p>The <strong>e-invoicing mandate</strong> requires businesses meeting specific criteria to implement electronic invoicing systems. Phase one requirements apply to most companies with annual revenues exceeding SAR 375,000, while phase two integration requirements depend on business size and transaction volumes.</p>
<p>VAT filing occurs quarterly or monthly depending on annual revenue thresholds. Late filing or payment triggers automatic penalties calculated daily on outstanding amounts. Maintaining accounting systems that properly track taxable supplies prevents compliance issues during ZATCA audits.</p>
<h3>Labour Platform Registration and Management</h3>
<p>The <strong>Muqeem platform</strong> manages expatriate worker visas, work permits, and exit-reentry permissions. Employers must maintain active accounts with sufficient visa quotas based on their business activity, capital, and Saudization compliance status.</p>
<p><strong>Qiwa platform</strong> consolidates Saudization monitoring, Nitaqat classification, and wage protection system compliance. Your company\'s Nitaqat color determines visa issuance eligibility and access to government services. Companies in red Nitaqat status face operational restrictions until improving Saudi national employment ratios.</p>
<p>The <strong>Wage Protection System (WPS)</strong> requires salary payments through approved banking channels by specific monthly deadlines. Late salary payments trigger automatic violations affecting your Nitaqat status and visa quota eligibility. Banks provide WPS salary file uploads facilitating compliant payment processing.</p>
This proactive approach prevents the violations that disrupt business operations and limit growth opportunities.</p>
<h3>Commercial Registration Maintenance and Amendments</h3>
<p><strong>Commercial registration certificates</strong> require renewal before expiration dates, typically annually or every five years depending on company type. Renewal involves updating <strong>articles of association</strong> if company information changed, refreshing trade name certificates, and verifying continuing business address validity.</p>
<p>Company structure changes require CR amendments before implementation. Adding business activities, changing share ownership, modifying capital, or appointing new managers all trigger mandatory amendment processes. Operating under outdated CR information creates legal liability during contract enforcement or regulatory inspections.</p>
<p>Branch registrations for companies operating across multiple cities require separate CRs for each location. Branches must maintain individual municipality licenses, Civil Defense approvals, and any sector-specific permits applicable to branch operations.</p>
<h2>Sector-Specific Incentives and Special Economic Zones</h2>
