<h2>Step-by-Step Ministry of Commerce Registration Process KSA</h2>
<p>The Ministry of Commerce operates a unified digital portal for all entity registrations. This portal replaced legacy paper-based systems in 2020, streamlining the process from 45 days to approximately 10-15 business days when documentation is complete.</p>
<h3>Step 1: Portal Access and Account Creation</h3>
<p>Access the Ministry of Commerce portal through the unified national gateway for government services. You\'ll need an active <strong>Absher account</strong> with business services enabled. Foreign investors without Saudi residency must designate a local service agent with Absher access to complete this step.</p>
<p>Create a new business application by selecting "Establish New Company" from the main dashboard. The system prompts you to select your business structure. Choose LLC, Joint Stock Company, or Sole Proprietorship based on your MISA license specifications.</p>
<p>Enter your reserved company name exactly as it appears on your Markaz certificate. The system validates the name against the Markaz database in real-time. Any discrepancy in spelling or format triggers an immediate rejection.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Company Information Entry</h3>
<p>Complete the company details form with information matching your MISA license and Articles of Association. This includes registered capital, business activities using the <strong>ISIC classification system</strong>, and registered office address. The address must be a physical commercial location with a valid <strong>Aqar certificate</strong>.</p>
<p>Specify your business activities using four-digit ISIC codes. Each activity requires corresponding authorization in your MISA license. Adding unauthorized activities triggers automatic application rejection. You may select up to five primary activities and unlimited secondary activities.</p>
<p>Enter shareholder information including nationality, ownership percentage, and capital contribution. For foreign shareholders, provide passport numbers and MISA reference codes. For Saudi shareholders, enter national ID numbers. Total ownership must equal exactly 100% with no rounding.</p>
<p>Motaded manages data entry across multiple government platforms simultaneously—ensuring perfect consistency between your MISA license, Markaz reservation, Ministry application, and Commercial Registration to prevent cross-platform rejections.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Document Upload</h3>
<p>Upload digital copies of all required documents in PDF format with maximum file size of 5MB per document. The portal requires specific document naming conventions: use only English characters without special symbols or Arabic numerals.</p>
<p><strong>Required uploads include:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>MISA Investment License</strong>: Upload the final approved license showing your company details</li>
<li><strong>Articles of Association</strong>: Upload the notarized version with attorney seal visible</li>
<li><strong>Name Reservation Certificate</strong>: Upload from Markaz showing active reservation</li>
<li><strong>Shareholder Identification</strong>: Passport copies for foreigners, national ID for Saudis</li>
<li><strong>Registered Address Proof</strong>: Aqar certificate or lease agreement for your office location</li>
<li><strong>Manager Authorization</strong>: Power of attorney if a non-shareholder will serve as company manager</li>
</ol>
<p>The system performs automated quality checks on uploaded documents. Illegible scans, cut-off pages, or missing signatures trigger immediate rejection notices. Resolution requirements exceed 300 DPI for all document scans.</p>
<h3>Step 4: Fee Payment and Submission</h3>
<p>Calculate your registration fees based on company type and declared capital. LLC registration requires a base fee of SAR 1,200 plus SAR 200 per shareholder. Joint Stock Companies incur fees of SAR 2,500 plus capital-based calculations.</p>
<p>Payment occurs through the integrated <strong>SADAD</strong> payment system. The portal generates a unique bill number valid for 72 hours. Payment can be completed through online banking, ATM, or bank branch. The system automatically updates your application status upon payment confirmation.</p>
<p>After payment verification, review your complete application in the summary screen. This is your final opportunity to identify errors before submission. Once submitted, applications cannot be edited—only withdrawn and resubmitted, which resets the processing timeline.</p>
<p>Submit your application and receive a unique tracking number. Save this number for status monitoring and future correspondence with the Ministry. The portal generates an email confirmation to all listed shareholder addresses.</p>
<h2>Ministry Review and Approval Timeline</h2>
<p>After submission, your application enters the Ministry\'s review queue. The standard processing timeline spans 10-15 business days for complete applications without deficiencies. Applications missing documents or containing inconsistencies face review suspension until corrections are submitted.</p>
<h3>Initial Automated Screening</h3>
<p>The Ministry\'s automated system performs the first review within 24 hours. This screening validates data consistency across linked government databases including MISA, Markaz, and the <strong>National Address system</strong>. Automated checks verify shareholder identities against immigration records and screen for business activity conflicts.</p>
<p>Applications passing automated screening advance to human reviewer assignment. Applications flagged by the system receive rejection notices with specific deficiency descriptions. Common automated rejections include shareholder identity mismatches, expired name reservations, and unauthorized business activities.</p>
<h3>Human Reviewer Assessment</h3>
<p>A Ministry officer reviews your application documents for compliance with Saudi Companies Law and sector-specific regulations. The reviewer examines your Articles of Association for required clauses, verifies authorized signatory appointments, and confirms registered capital adequacy for your declared activities.</p>
<p>Officers may request additional documentation or clarifications through the portal messaging system. You receive email notifications of such requests with deadlines typically set at 5 business days. Failure to respond within the deadline results in automatic application rejection.</p>
<p>For example, a foreign investor setting up an LLC in Riyadh for consulting services might receive requests for additional proof that all shareholders have completed required MISA steps if the license shows multiple investors but the application lists only one.</p>
<p>Motaded monitors your Ministry application throughout the review process—responding immediately to any reviewer requests and preventing deadline-triggered rejections that restart your entire timeline.</p>
<h3>Final Approval and Certificate Issuance</h3>
<p>Upon satisfactory review completion, the Ministry issues your <strong>Company Establishment Certificate</strong>. This certificate confirms your company\'s legal existence and authorizes you to proceed with Commercial Registration.</p>
<p>The certificate contains your Ministry reference number, approved company name in Arabic and English, registered capital, business activities, and shareholder details. This document serves as the foundation for all subsequent registration steps including CR issuance and tax registration.</p>
<p>Download your certificate from the portal immediately upon approval notification. The certificate carries a digital signature verifiable through the Ministry\'s authentication system. You\'ll need multiple copies for submission to various government entities during your formation process.</p>
<h2>Required Documentation Checklist</h2>
<p>Comprehensive documentation preparation prevents the majority of Ministry registration delays. The following checklist covers all required items with specific format requirements.</p>
<h3>Core Legal Documents</h3>
<p><strong>Articles of Association</strong>: Must include company name, registered capital, ownership percentages, business activities, management structure, shareholder rights and obligations, profit distribution mechanism, dispute resolution procedures, and dissolution terms. The document requires notarization by a licensed Saudi attorney with visible seal and signature. Pages must be numbered consecutively with corporate details in the header.</p>
<p><strong>MISA License</strong>: Must show active status with no pending conditions or post-approval requirements. The license must explicitly authorize the business activities you list in your Ministry application. Any deviation between MISA-approved activities and Ministry-submitted activities triggers automatic rejection.</p>
<p><strong>Name Reservation</strong>: Must show active reservation status with at least 30 days remaining until expiration. The name on the certificate must match exactly with the name in your Articles of Association and Ministry application—including spacing, hyphenation, and special characters.</p>
<h3>Shareholder Documentation</h3>
<p>Each shareholder must provide government-issued identification. Foreign shareholders submit passport copies showing personal information pages and Saudi entry stamps if currently in the Kingdom. Saudi shareholders provide national ID copies with visible expiration dates showing at least 6 months validity.</p>
<p>Shareholders who are companies (corporate shareholders) must provide commercial registration certificates from their home jurisdictions, board resolutions authorizing the Saudi investment, and beneficial ownership declarations identifying natural persons with 25% or greater ownership.</p>
<p>For shareholders appointing representatives to manage the company, provide notarized Power of Attorney documents specifying the scope of management authority granted to the representative.</p>
<h3>Registered Office Documentation</h3>
<p>The Ministry requires proof of your physical office location within Saudi Arabia. Acceptable proof includes Aqar property certificates for owned premises, notarized lease agreements showing minimum 12-month terms for leased offices, or facility management agreements for spaces within business centers.</p>
<p>The registered address must correlate with your business activities. Retail operations require ground-floor locations with street access. Industrial activities require premises within designated industrial zones. Service businesses may operate from executive offices or business centers.</p>
<p>Motaded secures compliant office spaces for clients through relationships with commercial landlords offering flexible terms and Ministry-approved documentation—eliminating one of the most common registration obstacles.</p>
<h2>Registration Fees and Payment Structure</h2>
<p>Ministry of Commerce registration involves multiple fee components based on business structure and declared capital. All fees are paid in Saudi Riyals through government-authorized payment channels.</p>
<h3>LLC Registration Fees (2026)</h3>
<p>Limited Liability Company registration requires a base fee of SAR 1,200 covering the establishment certificate issuance and initial database entry. Each shareholder beyond the first incurs an additional SAR 200 processing fee. Companies with five shareholders would pay SAR 2,000 total base fees.</p>
<p>Capital-based fees do not apply to LLCs for the Ministry registration itself, though you\'ll encounter capital-related fees during Commercial Registration. The Ministry requires proof of capital deposit only for companies with registered capital exceeding SAR 5 million.</p>
<h3>Joint Stock Company Fees</h3>
<p>Joint Stock Companies incur higher registration fees due to additional regulatory oversight requirements. The base fee reaches SAR 2,500 with additional charges calculated at 0.05% of declared capital for amounts exceeding SAR 1 million.</p>
<p>Joint Stock Companies must also pay board of directors registration fees of SAR 500 per board member, with minimum boards comprising three directors.</p>
<h3>Additional Service Fees</h3>
<p>Name reservation through Markaz (completed before Ministry registration) costs SAR 200 with 60-day validity. Name modifications after reservation require new reservations at the same fee.</p>
<p>Articles of Association notarization by licensed attorneys typically ranges from SAR 1,500 to SAR 3,500 depending on document complexity and shareholder count. This fee is not paid to the Ministry but to the notarizing attorney.</p>
