<p>Exempt supplies include residential property rentals and some financial services. Businesses making only exempt supplies cannot register for VAT and cannot reclaim input VAT on expenses. This distinction matters when structuring your <strong>business structure KSA 2026</strong> to optimize tax efficiency.</p>

<h2>Post-Registration Compliance Requirements</h2>
<p>Receiving your VAT certificate is only the beginning of your compliance obligations. ZATCA imposes ongoing requirements that apply from your registration date forward. Failure to meet these requirements triggers automatic penalties and may result in suspension of your VAT registration.</p>
<p>You must file VAT returns according to your assigned frequency—monthly, quarterly, or annually. ZATCA assigns filing frequency based on your annual taxable supplies. Businesses with supplies exceeding SAR 40 million must file monthly, while smaller businesses file quarterly. Each return must be submitted within 28 days after the end of the reporting period.</p>
<p>Tax invoices must meet specific ZATCA requirements to qualify as valid evidence for input VAT claims. Every invoice must include your TRN, customer details, itemized charges, and the VAT amount clearly stated. Electronic invoicing became mandatory in phases, and by 2026, all registered businesses must issue invoices through ZATCA-approved systems.</p>
<h3>Mandatory Compliance Actions</h3>
<ol>
<li>Issue compliant tax invoices for all taxable supplies</li>
<li>Maintain detailed accounting records for five years</li>
<li>Submit VAT returns by the deadline for each period</li>
<li>Pay any VAT due within the filing deadline</li>
<li>Update ZATCA within 30 days of any business changes</li>
<li>Undergo regular self-assessment for compliance gaps</li>
</ol>
<p>Record-keeping requirements are extensive and strict. You must retain all tax invoices, credit notes, customs declarations, import documents, and accounting records for five years from the end of the relevant tax period. ZATCA conducts regular audits and imposes significant penalties for inadequate record-keeping.</p>
