</table>
<p>Understanding these distinctions helps calculate your total contribution liability. Motaded\'s HR team configures your GOSI account according to your specific workforce composition, ensuring accurate monthly deductions from the start.</p>
<h2>Documents Required for GOSI Employer Registration</h2>
<p>Preparation accelerates the registration process. Gather all required documentation before accessing the <strong>GOSI online portal</strong>. Missing paperwork causes delays and forces resubmission cycles that postpone your compliance date.</p>
<p>You need your <strong>Commercial Registration certificate</strong> issued by the <strong>Ministry of Commerce</strong>. This document proves your business operates legally in Saudi Arabia. Include the CR number, issuance date, and activity classification in your application.</p>
<p>Provide a copy of the establishment contract or <strong>Articles of Association</strong> if you operate an LLC or corporation. This document confirms ownership structure and authorized signatories. Sole proprietorships submit the individual owner\'s national ID instead.</p>
<h3>Essential Registration Documents</h3>
<ol>
<li>Valid Commercial Registration certificate (original and copy)</li>
<li>Establishment contract or Articles of Association</li>
<li>National ID or Iqama for the company\'s authorized signatory</li>
<li>Bank account letter showing IBAN details for contribution payments</li>
<li>Employee list with names, nationalities, positions, and salaries</li>
<li>Employment contracts for each registered employee</li>
</ol>
<p>The authorized signatory must hold <strong>Absher Business</strong> credentials linked to the company\'s CR number. This digital identity enables portal access across all government platforms.
<h2>Step-by-Step GOSI Employee Registration Employer Guide</h2>
<p>The registration process follows a structured sequence through the official GOSI online portal. Each step builds on previous entries, creating your permanent employer profile in the national social insurance system. Follow this <strong>gosi employee registration employer process</strong> to avoid errors that trigger rejection.</p>
<h3>Step One: Create Your Employer Account</h3>
<p>Access the GOSI online services portal at gosi.gov.sa using your <strong>Absher Business</strong> credentials. Navigate to the employer registration section and select "New Establishment Registration." The system prompts you to enter your Commercial Registration number.</p>
<p>The portal retrieves your business details automatically from the <strong>Ministry of Commerce</strong> database. Verify the legal name, activity type, and registration address display correctly. Any discrepancies require correction at the Ministry of Commerce before proceeding.</p>
<h3>Step Two: Enter Establishment Information</h3>
<p>Complete the establishment profile form with operational details. Specify your business address, postal code, phone numbers, and email address. These contact points receive all official GOSI communications including payment notices and compliance alerts.</p>
<p>Select your primary economic activity from the dropdown menu. The classification determines your occupational hazard risk category, which affects contribution rates. Manufacturing and construction activities carry higher rates than administrative services or retail operations.</p>
<h3>Step Three: Register Your Employees</h3>
<p>Upload your employee roster using the provided Excel template. Each entry requires the employee\'s full name, national ID or Iqama number, nationality, date of birth, hiring date, job title, and monthly salary.</p>
<p>Saudi employees require additional fields: the branch selection for coverage (Occupational Hazards, Annuities, and SANED), previous employment history if transferring from another employer, and social insurance number if previously registered. Expatriate employees only enroll in Occupational Hazards coverage.</p>
<p>The system validates each employee\'s information against government databases. Duplicate registrations or expired identity documents trigger error messages. Correct all flagged entries before submitting the batch.</p>
<h3>Step Four: Calculate and Confirm Contributions</h3>
<p>GOSI automatically calculates monthly contribution amounts based on registered salaries and coverage branches. Review the contribution summary carefully. The Occupational Hazards Branch charges 2% of the employee\'s salary, paid entirely by the employer.</p>
<p>For Saudi nationals, the Annuities Branch requires 9% from the employer and 9% from the employee, totaling 18% of the monthly salary. The SANED unemployment insurance adds 1% from the employer and 1% from the employee.</p>
<h3>Step Five: Submit and Receive Your GOSI Number</h3>
<p>After reviewing all information, submit your registration application electronically. The system generates a unique GOSI establishment number immediately upon approval. This number identifies your business across all social insurance transactions.</p>
<p>Download and save your GOSI registration certificate. You need this document when applying for work visas through <strong>Muqeem</strong>, updating your <strong>Qiwa</strong> platform profile, and registering with <strong>ZATCA</strong> for tax purposes.
<h2>Understanding GOSI Contribution Rates and Calculations</h2>
<p>Calculating monthly GOSI contributions requires understanding the different branches and their respective rates. These percentages apply to the employee\'s gross monthly salary including basic pay, housing allowances, and transportation allowances.</p>
<p>The <strong>Occupational Hazards Branch</strong> covers workplace injuries and illnesses. Employers pay 2% of each employee\'s monthly salary. This rate applies to both Saudi nationals and expatriates. No employee contribution is required for this branch.</p>
<p>The <strong>Annuities Branch</strong> provides pension and disability benefits exclusively to Saudi citizens. Employers contribute 9% while employees contribute 9%, creating an 18% total deduction. This branch accumulates funds that pay retirement pensions after the employee reaches eligible age.</p>
