<h2>Annual Renewal Requirements and Process</h2>
<p>Chamber membership requires annual renewal tied to your Commercial Registration anniversary date. Automatic renewal systems don\'t exist—you must initiate renewal actively before expiration.</p>
<h3>Renewal Timeline and Deadlines</h3>
<p>Begin renewal procedures 60 days before membership expiration. The Chamber sends email reminders 90 days prior, but delivery isn\'t guaranteed due to system limitations. Mark renewal dates in your compliance calendar independently.</p>
<p>Operating with expired membership violates commercial regulations and may trigger fines during inspections. Some government platforms flag expired Chamber certificates and block transaction processing until renewal completion.</p>
<h3>Renewal Documentation</h3>
<p>Standard renewals require minimal documentation compared to initial registration. Update any changed information from the previous year:</p>
<ul>
<li>Updated Commercial Registration certificate if amended</li>
<li>New lease agreement if location changed</li>
<li>Updated bank letter if account switched</li>
<li>Manager changes reflected in new board resolutions</li>
</ul>
<p>Unchanged information rolls forward automatically. The system cross-references your CR status to verify continued active status before processing renewal.</p>
<h3>Renewal Fees and Payment</h3>
<p>Renewal fees match the annual membership rate based on current company capital. If you increased capital during the year, renewal fees adjust to the new tier automatically.</p>
<p>Pay through the same portal used for initial registration. Payment systems accept the same methods available during initial registration. Processing time for renewals averages three to five business days, significantly faster than initial applications.</p>
