<td>SAR 1</td>
<td>SAR 100,000 - 300,000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Trading Activities</td>
<td>SAR 1</td>
<td>SAR 500,000 - 1,000,000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Contracting</td>
<td>SAR 1</td>
<td>SAR 500,000 - 2,000,000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Manufacturing</td>
<td>SAR 1</td>
<td>SAR 1,000,000+</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>These capital requirements must be deposited into a Saudi bank account. The capital remains accessible after <strong>Commercial Registration CR Saudi Arabia</strong> issuance, typically within two weeks of deposit.</p>
<h3>Corporate Bank Account Opening</h3>
<p>Opening a corporate bank account presents its own cost structure. Saudi banks charge account opening fees ranging from SAR 500 to SAR 2,000 depending on the institution and account package selected.</p>
<p>Monthly account maintenance fees vary from SAR 100 to SAR 500. Premium account packages offering higher transaction limits and dedicated relationship managers cost more but provide valuable support for growing businesses.</p>
<p>Required minimum balances differ by bank, typically ranging from SAR 10,000 to SAR 50,000 for standard business accounts. These minimums don\'t represent fees but rather non-interest-bearing deposits that must remain in your account.</p>

<h2>Professional Service Provider Fees</h2>
<p>Most foreign investors engage professional consultants to navigate Saudi business formation requirements. These business setup cost in saudi arabia components vary widely based on service scope and provider expertise.</p>
<p>Full-service consultancies like Motaded offer comprehensive packages covering every registration step. Basic consultancy fees for LLC formation start at SAR 15,000 for straightforward cases with single-activity licenses and simple ownership structures.</p>
<h3>Business Setup Consultant Packages</h3>
<p>Consultant packages typically include MISA license application, AoA drafting, CR registration, initial <strong>ZATCA registration</strong> (Zakat, Tax and Customs Authority), and coordination with government entities. Mid-range packages cost SAR 25,000 to SAR 40,000 for standard LLC formation.</p>
<p>Premium services incorporate additional features like market entry strategy, location scouting, business plan development, and first-year compliance calendar management. Comprehensive packages range from SAR 50,000 to SAR 100,000 depending on business complexity.</p>
<p><strong>Key services included in consultant packages:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>MISA investment license application and follow-up</li>
<li>Articles of Association drafting and notarization coordination</li>
<li>Commercial Registration application and certificate collection</li>
<li>Initial ZATCA tax registration</li>
<li>Chamber of Commerce membership processing</li>
<li>Municipality license application support</li>
<li>Corporate bank account opening assistance</li>
</ol>
<p>The value proposition extends beyond simple document processing. Experienced consultants prevent costly mistakes in activity code selection, capital structure, and compliance framework establishment that could cause problems years later.
<h3>PRO Service Costs</h3>
<p><strong>Public Relations Officer (PRO)</strong> services handle government liaison work including visa processing, labor office registration, and ongoing document renewals. Companies can hire dedicated PRO staff or outsource to specialized firms.</p>
<p>Outsourced PRO services cost SAR 2,000 to SAR 5,000 monthly depending on service scope and transaction volume. For companies with minimal government interaction, pay-per-transaction models offer better value at SAR 500 to SAR 1,500 per visa or document processing.</p>
<p>Dedicated in-house PRO staff require monthly salaries of SAR 4,000 to SAR 8,000 plus benefits and sponsorship costs. This option makes financial sense only for larger organizations with continuous government transaction needs.</p>
<h2>Office Space and Infrastructure Costs</h2>
<p>Physical or virtual office space represents a significant ongoing expense category. Your <strong>business structure KSA 2026</strong> determines minimum space requirements, with some activities requiring physical facilities while others permit virtual arrangements.</p>
<p>Commercial office rental in Riyadh ranges from SAR 150 to SAR 400 per square meter annually depending on location and building quality. Jeddah rates run slightly lower at SAR 120 to SAR 350 per square meter.</p>
<h3>Physical Office Rental</h3>
<p>Prime business districts command premium rates. Riyadh\'s King Abdullah Financial District (KAFD) sees rates approaching SAR 400 per square meter. Olaya District offices range from SAR 250 to SAR 350 per square meter.</p>
<p>Secondary locations offer substantial savings. Industrial area offices in Riyadh cost SAR 100 to SAR 150 per square meter. These locations suit operations prioritizing function over prestige.</p>
<p><strong>Standard office lease terms:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Lease duration: Minimum one year, typically two to three years</li>
<li>Payment structure: Quarterly or semi-annual advance payments</li>
<li>Security deposit: One to two months rent</li>
<li>Fit-out period: Two to four weeks rent-free for tenant improvements</li>
<li>Renewal terms: Typically 5-10% increase every two years</li>
</ul>
<p>Most landlords require two months security deposit plus first quarter rent in advance. For a 100-square-meter office at SAR 200 per square meter annually, expect initial payment of approximately SAR 8,333 (SAR 5,000 quarterly rent plus SAR 3,333 security deposit).</p>
<h3>Virtual Office Solutions</h3>
<p>Virtual office arrangements satisfy <strong>Commercial Registration</strong> address requirements without physical space costs. Licensed business centers provide commercial addresses, mail handling, and occasional meeting room access for SAR 8,000 to SAR 18,000 annually.</p>
<p>Premium virtual office packages include dedicated phone answering services, professional reception, and regular meeting room hours. These comprehensive packages cost SAR 20,000 to SAR 35,000 annually but eliminate need for permanent physical space during initial operations.</p>

<h2>Employee Sponsorship and Visa Costs</h2>
<p>Hiring employees triggers additional cost layers including visa processing, medical examinations, insurance, and compliance with <strong>Nitaqat Saudization</strong> requirements. These represent ongoing operational costs but require initial setup as part of your launch budget.</p>
<p>Work visa processing costs SAR 2,000 per employee including visa issuance, entry permit, residence permit (iqama), and initial medical examination. Family dependent visas add SAR 1,500 per dependent for similar documentation.</p>
<h3>Expatriate Hiring Costs</h3>
<p>Beyond visa fees, companies must purchase mandatory health insurance for all expatriate employees. Basic coverage costs SAR 1,200 to SAR 2,000 per employee annually. Comprehensive coverage packages including dental and optical reach SAR 3,500 to SAR 5,000 per person annually.</p>
<p><strong>Iqama (residence permit)</strong> renewal runs SAR 650 per person annually. Exit-reentry permits allowing employees to travel cost SAR 200 for single exit or SAR 650 for multiple exit annually.</p>
<p>Initial employee setup including visa, medical examination, insurance, and labor office registration totals approximately SAR 5,000 to SAR 7,000 per expatriate worker. Budget accordingly when planning your founding team.</p>
<h3>Nitaqat Compliance Framework</h3>
<p>The Nitaqat system mandates Saudi national hiring quotas based on company size and activity sector. While Saudization doesn\'t directly create setup costs, understanding compliance requirements prevents expensive restructuring later.</p>
<p>Small entities with fewer than nine employees face no immediate Nitaqat obligations. Companies with ten or more employees must maintain Saudi-to-expatriate ratios ranging from 10% to 30% depending on activity classification.</p>
<p>Saudi national salaries typically start at SAR 5,000 to SAR 8,000 monthly for entry-level positions, significantly higher than comparable expatriate wages for similar roles.
<h2>Tax Registration and Compliance Setup</h2>
<p><strong>ZATCA registration</strong> marks a critical milestone carrying specific cost implications. While registration itself is free, compliance framework establishment requires professional support and software implementation.</p>
<p>Companies must register for multiple tax categories including Value Added Tax (VAT), withholding tax, and potentially excise tax depending on activities. Each registration carries distinct requirements and deadlines.</p>
<h3>VAT Registration Requirements</h3>
<p>Businesses with annual turnover exceeding SAR 375,000 must register for VAT. Voluntary registration is permitted for companies anticipating revenues above SAR 187,500. Registration involves comprehensive documentation of business activities, expected revenues, and transaction categories.</p>
<p>Most companies engage tax consultants for initial VAT registration, costing SAR 5,000 to SAR 12,000 including system setup, invoice template creation, and first-quarter compliance guidance. This investment prevents costly penalties for non-compliance or incorrect filing.</p>
<p><strong>VAT compliance requires:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>E-invoicing system implementation meeting ZATCA technical specifications</li>
<li>Detailed revenue and expense tracking by transaction category</li>
<li>Quarterly VAT return filing</li>
<li>Annual reconciliation and reporting</li>
<li>Maintenance of supporting documentation for six years</li>
</ol>
<p>E-invoicing implementation represents an additional cost layer. ZATCA-compliant software costs SAR 3,000 to SAR 10,000 for implementation plus SAR 500 to SAR 2,000 monthly subscription fees depending on transaction volume and features required.</p>
<h3>Zakat and Corporate Tax</h3>
<p>Saudi-owned businesses pay Zakat at 2.5% of adjusted zakat base annually. Foreign-owned entities pay corporate income tax at 20% on taxable profits. Mixed ownership companies split obligations proportionally.</p>
<p>Initial tax registration and first-year compliance setup through professional accountants costs SAR 8,000 to SAR 15,000. This covers ZATCA registration, accounting system configuration, chart of accounts setup aligned with Saudi tax requirements, and guidance on deductible expenses and documentation requirements.</p>
<p>Ongoing annual tax compliance including return preparation and filing runs SAR 12,000 to SAR 30,000 annually depending on business complexity and transaction volume. These costs sit outside immediate setup budget but merit consideration in first-year financial planning.</p>

<h2>Business Setup Cost in Saudi Arabia by Company Type</h2>
<p>Total setup costs vary dramatically by chosen business structure. This section provides realistic budget ranges for common entity types, incorporating all fee categories discussed above.</p>
<p>Understanding comparative costs helps you select the optimal structure balancing legal protection, operational flexibility, and budget constraints. The business setup cost in saudi arabia differs significantly between wholly foreign-owned LLCs, joint venture arrangements, and branch office structures.</p>
<h3>Limited Liability Company (LLC) Formation</h3>
<p>LLC formation represents the most popular choice for foreign investors seeking operational flexibility and limited liability protection. Total setup costs for a straightforward single-shareholder LLC typically range from SAR 45,000 to SAR 85,000.</p>
<p><strong>LLC cost breakdown:</strong></p>
