<h3>Free Zone Registration Cost Breakdown</h3>
<p>Free zone costs concentrate around the license and mandatory space rental. King Abdullah Economic City requires minimum office commitments starting at SAR 30,000 annually for a flexi-desk. Jazan Economic City offers lower entry points around SAR 18,000 for small service businesses.</p>
<p>Setup costs include zone registration fees typically between SAR 5,000 and SAR 15,000. This covers initial processing and license issuance. Some zones charge separate establishment card fees for each employee, adding SAR 500 to SAR 1,000 per person annually.</p>
<p>Additional costs include utility deposits if you lease dedicated office space. Security deposits often equal three months\' rent. These upfront cash requirements significantly impact your working capital planning.</p>
<h2>Capital Requirements and Deposit Obligations</h2>
<p>Capital requirements differ substantially between free zones and mainland. Mainland LLCs must declare minimum capital in the <strong>articles of association</strong>. While no universal minimum exists, practical requirements start at SAR 100,000 for service companies and SAR 500,000 for certain regulated activities.</p>
<p>This declared capital must be deposited in a blocked bank account before CR issuance. The funds remain frozen until the company is fully registered. Banks require proof of capital transfer as part of account opening procedures.</p>
<p>Free zones impose different capital rules. Many allow lower declared capital, sometimes as low as SAR 10,000 for consultancy services. However, you still need working capital to cover mandatory lease commitments and employee salaries during the startup phase.</p>
<h3>Bank Account Opening Process and Costs</h3>
<p>Opening a corporate bank account follows different procedures based on your structure. Mainland companies present their CR, MISA license, and articles of association to Saudi banks. Account opening takes one to three weeks and may require minimum balance commitments between SAR 50,000 and SAR 500,000 depending on the bank.</p>
<p>Free zone companies must provide zone registration certificates and office lease agreements. Some banks classify these as higher-risk accounts, requiring larger deposits. International banks operating in Saudi Arabia often prefer mainland structures for account relationships.</p>

<h2>Office Space and Physical Presence Requirements</h2>
<p>Office requirements represent one of the largest ongoing cost differences. Mainland companies must maintain a physical office address listed on the CR. You can choose from executive offices, co-working spaces, or traditional leases based on your budget.</p>
<p>Annual lease costs in Riyadh for a furnished executive office start around SAR 40,000 for a single-room setup. Co-working memberships cost SAR 18,000 to SAR 36,000 annually. These spaces must be in commercially zoned buildings approved by the municipality.
<p>Free zones mandate office space within the zone boundaries. This eliminates location flexibility but simplifies compliance. Zone offices include infrastructure like internet, utilities, and security. However, total annual costs often exceed mainland equivalents when factoring in mandatory minimums.</p>
