      'body' => '<h1>Notary and Legal Documentation Costs in Saudi Arabia</h1>
<p>Setting up a business in Saudi Arabia involves more than licensing and registration fees. <strong>Notary documentation fees Saudi Arabia</strong> businesses face represent a significant yet frequently underestimated expense category. Foreign investors often budget for obvious costs like <strong>MISA</strong> applications and <strong>Commercial Registration</strong>, but overlook the cumulative impact of notarisation, translation, attestation, and legal document preparation.</p>
<p>Understanding these documentation costs upfront prevents budget overruns during critical formation stages. This guide breaks down every notary and legal documentation expense you\'ll encounter when establishing your Saudi entity in 2026.</p>
<h2>What Notary and Legal Documentation Costs Cover</h2>
<p>Notary and documentation expenses span multiple mandatory processes throughout business formation. These costs accumulate across three distinct phases: pre-registration document preparation, official government submissions, and post-registration compliance filings.</p>
<p><strong>Pre-registration costs</strong> include drafting your <strong>Articles of Association</strong>, translating foreign documents, and obtaining home country attestations. <strong>Submission costs</strong> cover notarising signature pages, certifying true copies, and authenticating corporate resolutions. <strong>Post-registration costs</strong> involve translating licences for banking purposes and notarising employment contracts.</p>
<p>Motaded handles the entire documentation workflow from initial drafting through final attestation—motaded.com.sa ensures nothing delays your company launch.</p>
<h3>Document Types Requiring Notarisation</h3>
<p>Saudi authorities require notarised documentation for <strong>LLC formation Saudi Arabia</strong> processes and branch office registrations. Passport copies, corporate resolutions, bank reference letters, and authorisation letters all need notary certification before submission.</p>
<p>Commercial documents like <strong>Articles of Association Saudi Arabia</strong> entities use must carry notary seals. Memorandums of Association, shareholder agreements, and capital contribution declarations require both notarisation and translation when originating from foreign jurisdictions.</p>
<p>Power of attorney documents granting local representation rights demand notarisation in your home country plus Saudi embassy attestation. Lease agreements for registered office addresses similarly require notary authentication before <strong>Ministry of Commerce Saudi Arabia</strong> acceptance.</p>
<h3>Translation and Attestation Requirements</h3>
<p>Every foreign-language document submitted to Saudi authorities requires certified Arabic translation. Translation costs vary based on document length, complexity, and turnaround urgency. Standard passport translations range from SAR 100-200 per document, while complex corporate documents cost SAR 300-800 depending on page count.</p>
<p>Attestation follows a chain process: notarisation in your home country, authentication by your foreign ministry, then legalisation by the Saudi embassy or consulate. Some countries participating in the Hague Apostille Convention simplify this to apostille certification plus Saudi embassy legalisation.</p>
<p>Working with Motaded at this stage means your documents follow the correct attestation sequence the first time—motaded.com.sa coordinates with notaries and embassies across 40+ countries.</p>
