      'body' => '<h1>MISA License Fee Types 2026 Breakdown for Construction Companies in Saudi Arabia</h1>
<p>Construction companies entering Saudi Arabia face a dual-approval framework that sets this sector apart. Unlike trading or consulting firms that only need a <strong>MISA</strong> license, construction businesses must secure both <strong>Ministry of Investment of Saudi Arabia (MISA)</strong> approval and a concurrent <strong>Ministry of Municipal and Rural Affairs (MOMRA)</strong> classification. Understanding the misa license fee types 2026 breakdown prevents costly delays and ensures compliance with both regulatory bodies from project inception.</p>
<p>Foreign investors typically underestimate the timeline and documentation required for construction-specific licensing. The process involves technical capacity verification, financial threshold assessments, and safety protocol evaluations that other sectors bypass entirely. This guide breaks down every fee component, approval stage, and document requirement specific to construction companies establishing operations in the Kingdom.</p>
<h2>Why Construction Companies Need Dual Regulatory Approval</h2>
<p>Construction firms cannot operate on MISA authorization alone. <strong>MOMRA</strong> classification determines which project types and contract values your company can legally bid on. This classification system—ranging from Grade Five to Grade One—directly impacts revenue potential and market positioning within Saudi Arabia\'s booming infrastructure sector.</p>
<p>The dual requirement stems from public safety considerations and quality assurance protocols. MOMRA evaluates technical competency through equipment ownership, past project portfolios, and qualified personnel ratios. MISA focuses on investment capital, business structure compliance, and foreign ownership regulations under Saudi company law.</p>

<h3>How MOMRA Classification Affects Market Access</h3>
<p>Your MOMRA grade determines maximum contract values per project. Grade Five contractors handle contracts up to SAR 3 million. Grade One firms bid on unlimited-value infrastructure projects including roads, bridges, and government complexes. Most foreign entrants target Grade Three initially, permitting contracts up to SAR 30 million while building local project history.</p>
<p>Classification upgrades require demonstrated project completion, equipment acquisition, and increased paid-up capital. Companies planning expansion into mega-projects must factor reclassification costs into long-term financial projections and business structure KSA 2026 planning.</p>
<h2>MISA License Fee Types 2026 Breakdown: Construction Sector Costs</h2>
<p>The misa license fee types 2026 breakdown for construction companies includes base registration fees, sector-specific approvals, and MOMRA classification charges. Base <strong>MISA investment license saudi arabia</strong> registration starts at SAR 1,200 for <strong>Limited Liability Companies (LLC)</strong> structures. This covers initial application processing and digital certificate issuance through the Ministry\'s online portal.</p>
<p>Construction-specific additions include technical file review (SAR 800), equipment verification documentation (SAR 500), and safety protocol assessment (SAR 600). These fees apply regardless of chosen MOMRA grade and represent mandatory compliance checkpoints before classification assignment.</p>
<p><strong>MOMRA</strong> classification fees operate on a sliding scale. Grade Five classification costs SAR 2,000 annually. Grade Four requires SAR 5,000. Grade Three—the most common foreign entrant tier—costs SAR 10,000 yearly. Higher grades increase proportionally, with Grade One contractors paying SAR 50,000 annually for classification maintenance.</p>
