<h3>Additional Compliance Costs for Construction Firms</h3>
<p>Beyond licensing fees, construction companies face mandatory <strong>commercial registration CR saudi arabia</strong> costs through the <strong>Ministry of Commerce</strong>. CR issuance for construction entities costs SAR 1,600 with annual renewal fees of SAR 800. This registration enables contract signing, bank account opening, and labour recruitment.</p>
<p><strong>ZATCA registration</strong> for VAT purposes carries no direct fee but requires tax agent appointment for companies exceeding SAR 375,000 annual revenue. Most construction firms surpass this threshold immediately, adding SAR 8,000–12,000 annually for compliant tax filing services.</p>
<h2>Step-by-Step MISA and MOMRA Application Process</h2>
<p>Construction company formation follows a precise sequence that interweaves MISA approval with MOMRA classification. Deviation from this order creates regulatory conflicts requiring application restarts.</p>
<h3>Step 1: Reserve Company Name and Initial Investment License</h3>
<p>Begin with name reservation through MISA\'s digital platform. Construction company names must avoid restricted terms and comply with Arabic naming conventions. Submit three name preferences with English transliterations. Approval takes 2–3 business days.</p>
<p>Simultaneously apply for the initial investment license through MISA\'s portal. Upload passport copies, investment intent declaration, and preliminary business plan indicating construction focus. Initial approval grants 180 days for completing subsequent steps.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Draft Articles of Association Reflecting Construction Activities</h3>
<p><strong>Articles of association saudi arabia</strong> for construction firms must specify permissible project types under Saudi classification codes. Include civil construction, building construction, specialized trades, and project management as separate activity codes to maximize MOMRA classification options.</p>
<p>Define capital structure meeting both MISA foreign ownership rules and MOMRA minimum capital requirements per classification grade. Grade Three requires minimum SAR 2 million paid-up capital. Grade Two demands SAR 4 million. Structure shareholder agreements to accommodate future grade upgrades without complete document redrafting.</p>
